"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not found a complete solution for arbitrary\u202f\\(n\\).  \nI have, however, rigorously proved the exact set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) for the smallest non\u2011trivial case \\(n=3\\) and corrected the mistake in the previous construction for \\(k=2\\).  \nIn particular I have shown that for \\(n=3\\) the only possible values are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction for \\(k=0\\) works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); the general classification of admissible\u202f\\(k\\) for larger\u202f\\(n\\) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The condition of the problem is that we must find \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (always possible\u202f\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_a\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is admissible for every \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Complete analysis for \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains six points.  \n   Because we have exactly three lines, at least one line must contain at least two of the points.\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) cover all six points.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the two non\u2011sunny vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) and the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52 .\n   \\]\n\n   The incidences are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point}&\\text{covering line(s)}\\\\\\hline\n   (1,1)&x=1\\\\\n   (1,2)&x=1,\\;L\\\\\n   (1,3)&x=1\\\\\n   (2,1)&x=2\\\\\n   (2,2)&x=2\\\\\n   (3,1)&L\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines and exactly one line (\\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&: y=x,\\\\\n   L_2&: y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\\\\n   L_3&: y=-2x+5\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   cover \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) as follows  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point}&\\text{covering line(s)}\\\\\\hline\n   (1,1)&L_1\\\\\n   (1,2)&L_2\\\\\n   (1,3)&L_3\\\\\n   (2,1)&L_3\\\\\n   (2,2)&L_1\\\\\n   (3,1)&L_2\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   All six points are covered and every line is sunny.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Suppose three distinct lines contain the six points and exactly two of them are sunny.  \n   Because a non\u2011sunny line can be only vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), any non\u2011sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) (the three points with the same \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate).  \n   If the unique non\u2011sunny line is vertical, say \\(x=1\\), then the remaining points \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) must be covered by two sunny lines.  \n   The only sunny line that contains \\((3,1)\\) is the line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\), namely \\(y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52\\).  \n   The two points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) cannot both lie on a single sunny line (the line through them is vertical, which is non\u2011sunny).  \n   Hence at least three sunny lines would be required, contradicting the assumption \\(k=2\\).  \n   The same argument works if the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n   Combining the four items we obtain the complete list for \\(n=3\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Remark on the general case.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n   The constructions for \\(k=1\\) (the one used for \\(n=3\\)) and for \\(k=n\\) (the three\u2011sunny construction for \\(n=3\\)) suggest that many intermediate values of \\(k\\) may be possible, but a systematic method that works for every \\(n\\) has not been found.  \n   Determining the full set of admissible \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\) therefore remains an open problem."

