k gives two explicit sunny lines that cover all newly added points and intersect none of the previously used vertical lines.\n\n   Finally we keep the original family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) (which uses only points with abscissa \\(\\le m\\)) and add the two new sunny lines.  \n   The total number of lines is \\((m)+2=m+2\\) and all of them are sunny.  \n   Hence a covering with exactly \\(m+2\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   Because the induction step adds two lines, the construction works for every odd \\(m\\) (starting from the base case \\(m=1\\)) and for every even \\(m\\) (starting from the base case \\(m=2\\)).  \n   Consequently, for **every** integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \n   Take \\(k\\) arbitrary with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Use the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines as in step\u202f5; the remaining points form a copy of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).  \n   By step\u202f6 we can cover this copy with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is realizable.\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is even.*  \n   The same construction works for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n   For \\(k=n\\) it would require a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines, which is impossible by the capacity inequality (2) because for even \\(n\\) we have \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor =\\frac n2\\) and then the left\u2011hand side of (2) equals \\(\\frac{n^2}{2}<N_n\\).  \n   Therefore \\(k=n\\) is excluded, while all smaller \\(k\\) are attainable as described.\n\n   This completes the classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For even }n\\ge 3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\};\\qquad\n       \\text{For odd }n\\ge 3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n