            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximal number of points that a line can contain\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \nThe same bound holds for horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) and for lines of slope \\(-1\\) (the lines \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nWrite the slope in lowest terms \\(\\frac pq\\) with \\(p,q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on such a line have the form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+ tq,\\;y_0+ tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe sum \\(x+y\\) increases by \\(p+q\\) when \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).  \nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to the three forbidden directions, we have \\(p+q\\ge 2\\); consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any sunny line meets }\\mathcal P_n\\text{ in at most }\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nUsing the maximal capacities (6) for sunny lines and the bound \\(n\\) for non‑sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\nkM_n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N_n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and \\(N_n\\) from (3).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(M_n=t\\) and (7) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk t+(2t-k)2t\\ge 2t(2t+1)/2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M_n=t+1\\) and (7) yields  \n\n\\[\nk(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1)\\ge (2t+1)(2t+2)/2,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to the identity \\(k\\le 2t+1=n\\).  \nHence the inequality does **not** forbid the case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A universal construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the unique line \\(x=a\\).  \nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nUse the first \\(n-k\\) vertical lines from (9); they cover all points whose abscissa belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  \nThe uncovered points are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nDefine the affine map  \n\n\\[\n\\phi:\\;(x,y)\\longmapsto (x-(n-k),\\,y-(n-k)).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\phi\\) sends \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) bijectively onto \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).  \nTherefore, to finish the construction it suffices to cover \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) **sunny** lines.\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following sub‑problem.\n\n> **Sub‑problem.** For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) find a family of exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines whose union contains \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Solving the sub‑problem\n\nWe prove the sub‑problem by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n#### Base cases  \n\n* \\(m=1\\): \\(\\mathcal P_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n\n* \\(m=2\\): \\(\\mathcal P_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  The two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\n  \\tag{12}\n  \\]\n\n  cover the three points, and both have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 2\\) we have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nof \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).  \nWe construct a family for \\(m+2\\).  \n(The induction adds two lines, so it works simultaneously for the odd and the even subsequences.)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nConsider the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+2}\\) whose abscissa exceeds \\(t\\); explicitly,\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal P_{m+2}\\mid x>t\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection shows that after translating by \\((-t,-t)\\) the set \\(\\mathcal R\\) becomes exactly \\(\\mathcal P_2\\):\n\\[\n\\phi_t(x,y)=(x-t,\\;y-t)\\quad\\text{maps }\\mathcal R\\text{ onto }\\mathcal P_2 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence the two sunny lines displayed in (12) (translated back by \\((t,t)\\)) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal R\\).  \nDenote these translated lines by \\(\\widetilde L_{m+1},\\widetilde L_{m+2}\\).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+2}\\) whose abscissa is at most \\(t\\) belong to the set \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) after the same translation; consequently they are covered by the original family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+2}:=\\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{\\widetilde L_{m+1},\\widetilde L_{m+2}\\}\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\((m)+2=m+2\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the whole \\(\\mathcal P_{m+2}\\).\n\nThe induction is complete, and we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma 5.1.** For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nChoose any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nUse the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (9) and, by Lemma 5.1, cover the remaining copy of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nHence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is realizable.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nThe same construction works for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nFor \\(k=n\\) it would require a covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines, which contradicts inequality (8).  \nTherefore the admissible values are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd:  } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
