ny line meets }\\mathcal P_n\\text{ in at most }\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points}.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that must be left for the sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nand \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nTo maximise the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines we may take\nthe vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise disjoint inside \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); any other family of\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover **no more** distinct points, because each\ncolumn of the triangular array \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is maximal and the columns are\nnested.\n\nThe number of points covered by the lines (4) is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-c)\n   =N_n-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence at least  \n\n\\[\nR_k:=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain uncovered; the uncovered set is a translate of\n\\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality for the sunny lines  \n\nInside the uncovered set each sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nM_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints (apply (3) with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\)).  \nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\,M_k\\;\\ge\\;R_k\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is **even**, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k}{2}\\) and\nthe left‑hand side of (8) equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\), whereas the right‑hand side\nequals \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); the inequality fails.  \nConsequently **no even \\(k\\ge 2\\) is possible**.\n\nAssume now that \\(k\\) is **odd**, say \\(k=2t+1\\).  Then \\(M_k=t+1\\) and (8) becomes an\nequality:\n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)