(a_0+ tq,\\;b_0+ tp)\\) is valid only for **positive** slopes \\(p/q>0\\).  Sunny lines may have **negative** slopes (e.g. \\(-2\\)), which would be written as \\(y = -\\frac{p}{q}x + c\\) and have lattice points of the form \\((a_0+ tq,\\;b_0- tp)\\).  The solution completely omits this case, so the subsequent argument does not apply to all sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Maximal number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on a sunny line \u2013 **second part**\n> \u201cWhen \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) increases by \\(p+q\\).  \n> Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the number of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{p+q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n> Because a sunny line is not parallel to the three forbidden directions, \\(p+q\\ge 2\\); therefore any sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.**  \nThe claim that \u201c\\(a+b\\) increases by \\(p+q\\)\u201d is true only for **positive** slopes.  For a line of negative slope \\(-p/q\\) the sum changes by \\(q-p\\), which can be **smaller** in absolute value (e.g. for slope \\(-2\\) we have \\(q-p=-1\\)).  Consequently the derived bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{p+q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) does **not** apply to those lines.  Although the final numerical bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) happens to be true for all sunny lines, the solution never proves it for the negative\u2011slope case; the argument is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Choice of non\u2011sunny lines\n> \u201cTo maximise the number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines we may take the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n> These lines are pairwise disjoint inside \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); any other family of \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover **no more** distinct points, because each column of the triangular array \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is maximal and the columns are nested.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.**  \nThe statement that the vertical family is optimal is plausible but not proved.  A rigorous argument would need to show that any other selection of \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (including horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) lines) cannot exceed the total \\(\\sum_{c=1}^{n-k}(n+1-c)\\) of distinct points.  The solution merely sketches the idea (\u201ccolumns are maximal and nested\u201d) without a formal proof, leaving a gap.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Number of points left for sunny lines\n> \u201cThe number of points covered by the lines (4) is  \n> \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-c)=N_n-\\frac{k(k+1)}2.\\)  \n> Hence at least \\(R_k:=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain uncovered; the uncovered set is a translate of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\u201d\n\n*Given the (unproved) optimality of the vertical family, the arithmetic is correct.*  The uncovered set indeed consists of the rightmost \\(k\\) columns, which after translation is exactly \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Capacity of sunny lines inside the uncovered set\n> \u201cInside the uncovered set each sunny line can contain at most  \n> \\(M_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points (apply (3) with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\)).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.**  \nThis step relies on the bound (3) proved only for positive slopes.  Since the bound for negative slopes has not been established, the claim that *every* sunny line is limited to \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points in the translated triangle is not justified.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Necessary inequality for the sunny lines\n> \u201cTherefore we must have \\(k\\,M_k\\;\\ge\\;R_k\\) i.e.  \n> \\(k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2.\\)\u201d\n\n*Assuming the previous bound, the inequality follows correctly.*  The algebraic manipulation is sound.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Even \\(k\\) are impossible\n> \u201cIf \\(k\\