or a fixed \\(c\\) the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) lying on the line \\(y=x+c\\) equals  \n> \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-|c|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor.\\)  \n> The value \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}=t+1\\) is attained **only for \\(c=0\\)**.  \n> Hence at most **one** of the lines (10) can contain the required \\(t+1\\) points; the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines would contain strictly fewer points, contradicting the equality in (9).  Therefore **no odd \\(k\\ge 3\\) is possible**.\u201d\n\n**Critical Error (propagated).**  Because step\u202f12 is false, the restriction to lines \\(y=x+c\\) is unwarranted.  In reality, one can use a mixture of slopes (e.g. \\(1\\), \\(-2\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) to achieve the required point counts, as shown by explicit constructions (see below).  Hence the conclusion that odd \\(k\\ge3\\) is impossible is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 14.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n> \u201c\\(k=0\\). The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=i\\) ( \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) ) are distinct, none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n> \\(k=1\\). Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\). All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered except \\((n,1)\\). Add any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance \\(L:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1\\).\u201d\n\n*Both constructions are correct* and indeed show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 15.  Final answer\n> \u201cFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  The answer is **incorrect**.  A concrete counterexample exists for \\(k=3\\) (and, by similar constructions, for any odd \\(k\\)).  For instance, with \\(n=5\\) and \\(k=3\\) one can use the following \\(5\\) distinct lines:\n\n* Non\u2011sunny lines: \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) (cover the left two columns).  \n* Sunny lines (cover the remaining triangle \\(\\mathcal P_3\\)):\n  1. \\(y = x-2\\)