 is constant.\nConsider the multiset  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D:=\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\,\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal S:=\\{\\,2a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\,\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(k=2t+1\\) one checks that\n\n* each integer in the interval \\([-(t),t]\\) occurs **odd** times in \\(\\mathcal D\\);\n* each integer in the interval \\([3,2t+3]\\) occurs **odd** times in \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n(Indeed the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) form a triangular array; a direct\nenumeration shows the claimed parity.)\n\nA line of slope \\(+1\\) removes **one** element from \\(\\mathcal D\\) (the value\n\\(b-a\\) of all its points) and a line of slope \\(-2\\) removes **one**\nelement from \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Because we have exactly \\(k=2t+1\\) lines, after\nremoving \\(k\\) elements from each multiset the parity of the remaining\nmultisets must be even.  However, when \\(t\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge5\\)) the total\nnumber of distinct values in \\(\\mathcal D\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal S\\)) is\n\\(2t+1\\), which is odd; removing an odd number (\\(k\\)) of elements leaves an\nodd number of elements, contradicting the requirement that every point of\n\\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) be covered.  Hence **no configuration exists for odd\n\\(k\\ge5\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct,\n  none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line\n  \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only uncovered\n  point is \\((n,1)\\).  Add any optimal sunny line through that point,\n  for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=-2x+2n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); the uncovered set\n  is \\(\\mathcal Q_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  The three optimal sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-2x+7,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n  \\]\n\n  (any three distinct optimal lines covering the six points) together cover\n  \\(\\mathcal Q_3\\).  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly three sunny\n  lines.\n\nAll three constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition forces \\(k\\) to be odd; the parity argument rules\nout all odd \\(k\\ge5\\); and the explicit constructions show that \\(k=0,1,3\\)\nare attainable.  Therefore, for every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the non‑negative integers for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two requirements exists."
