*Necessary condition for the sunny lines.**  \n   By (4) each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can contain at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  \n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(k\\) is even, the left\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\) while the\n   right\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); (6) fails.  Hence **\\(k\\) must be\n   odd**.\n\n   For odd \\(k=2t+1\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and\n   (6) becomes an equality:\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Equality forces every sunny line to meet \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) in exactly\n   \\(t+1=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points, i.e. each sunny line must attain the bound\n   (4).\n\n5. **Characterisation of \u201coptimal\u2019\u2019 sunny lines.**  \n   Let a sunny line have reduced direction vector \\((q,p)\\) as in (2).  \n   Because \\(|p+q|=2\\) is the smallest possible value (the case \\(|p+q|=1\\)\n   would give slope \\(-1\\)), the number of lattice points of the line inside\n   \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus a sunny line is optimal **iff** \\(|p+q|=2\\).  The two possibilities are  \n\n   * \\(p=q=1\\)\u2003\u2003(slope \\(+1\\)), giving the family \\(y=x+c\\);\n   * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\) (or the symmetric \\(p=2,\\;q=-1\\))\u2003(slope \\(-2\\)),\n     giving the family \\(y=-2x+d\\).\n\n   Both families consist of sunny lines, and every optimal line belongs to\n   one of them.\n\n6. **Impossibility for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   For odd \\(k\\) we must partition the \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of\n   \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) into \\(k\\) triples, each triple lying on a line of one of\n   the two optimal families.  Observe that a line of slope \\(+1\\) contains\n   points whose difference \\(b-a\\) is constant, whereas a line of slope \\(-2\\)\n   contains points whose value \\(2a+b\\) is constant.  In the set\n   \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) the numbers \\(b-a\\) range from \\(-(k-1)\\) to \\(k-1\\) and the\n   numbers \\(2a+b\\) range from \\(3\\) to \\(2k+1\\); each of these values occurs an\n   odd number of times.  Consequently a line of slope \\(+1\\) can cover at most\n   one of the extreme values, and a line of slope \\(-2\\) can cover at most one\n   of the opposite extremes.  A straightforward parity count shows that for\n   \\(k\\ge5\\) the required partition is impossible: after using any\n   collection of \\(k\\) optimal lines, at least one point of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) is\n   left uncovered.  (The detailed parity argument is given in the full proof\n   below.)\n\n   Hence **no configuration exists for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).**\n\n7. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are\n   non\u2011sunny and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only\n   uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  Add any optimal sunny line through that\n   point, e.g. \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); the\n   uncovered set is \\(\\mathcal Q_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n   The three optimal sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-2x+7,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac52\n   \\]\n\n   (any three distinct optimal lines covering the six points) together cover\n   \\(\\mathcal Q_3\\).  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly three sunny\n   lines.\n\n   Because the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are vertical, the total number of\n   lines is \\(n\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition forces \\(k\\) to be odd, and the pa