Assume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nAmong the non\u2011sunny lines the vertical ones are the most \u201cefficient\u2019\u2019:\nthe column \\(x=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and the\ncolumns are nested.  Therefore the maximal number of points that can be\ncovered by \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines is obtained by taking the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-c)=N_n-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints, leaving exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_k:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal Q_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Geometrically \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) is a translate of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nEvery sunny line can meet \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\nM_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints, by (4) applied with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\,M_k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal Q_k|\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, the left\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\) while the\nright\u2011hand side equals \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); (11) fails.  Therefore **\\(k\\) must\nbe odd**.\n\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge0\\)).  Then \\(M_k=t+1\\) and (11) becomes an equality:\n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must be *optimal*, i.e. it must\nbelong to one of the families (6) and contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Parity obstruction for \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nFor an optimal line of slope \\(+1\\) the quantity \\(b-a\\)