            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n:=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points on a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced form  \n\n\\[\ny-y_0=\\frac{p}{q}(x-x_0),\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;\n\\gcd(|p|,|q|)=1,\\;p+q\\neq0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll its lattice points are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+ tq,\\;y_0+ tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(|p+q|\\).  \nBecause the forbidden slope \\(-1\\) corresponds to \\(p+q=0\\), for a sunny line\n\\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  Consequently, inside the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the\nparameter \\(t\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ndifferent values, i.e. a sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most the\nright‑hand side of (4).  Equality in (4) occurs exactly when \\(|p+q|=2\\); the\ntwo possibilities are  \n\n\\[\n(p,q)=(1,1)\\quad\\text{(slope }+1\\text{)}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad(p,q)=(-2,1)\n\\quad\\text{(slope }-2\\text{)} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line is **optimal** (contains the maximal possible number of\npoints) iff it belongs to one of the families  \n\n\\[\nL^{(+)}_c:\\;y=x+c,\\qquad\nL^{(-)}_d:\\;y=-2x+d .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the small triangle \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\)\n\nAssume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nAmong the non‑sunny lines the vertical ones are the most “efficient’’:\nthe column \\(x=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and the\ncolumns are nested.  Therefore the maximal number of points that can be\ncovered by \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is obtained by taking the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-c)=N_n-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints, leaving exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_k:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal Q_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Geometrically \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) is a translate of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nEvery sunny line can meet \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\nM_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints, by (4) applied with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\,M_k\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal Q_k|\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, the left‑hand side equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\) while the\nright‑hand side equals \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\); (11) fails.  Therefore **\\(k\\) must\nbe odd**.\n\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge0\\)).  Then \\(M_k=t+1\\) and (11) becomes an equality:\n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nConsequently each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines must be *optimal*, i.e. it must\nbelong to one of the families (6) and contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Parity obstruction for \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nFor an optimal line of slope \\(+1\\) the quantity \\(b-a\\) is constant; for\nan optimal line of slope \\(-2\\) the quantity \\(2a+b\\) is constant.\nConsider the multiset  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D:=\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\,\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal S:=\\{\\,2a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_k\\,\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(k=2t+1\\) one checks that\n\n* each integer in the interval \\([-(t),t]\\) occurs **odd** times in \\(\\mathcal D\\);\n* each integer in the interval \\([3,2t+3]\\) occurs **odd** times in \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n(Indeed the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) form a triangular array; a direct\nenumeration shows the claimed parity.)\n\nA line of slope \\(+1\\) removes **one** element from \\(\\mathcal D\\) (the value\n\\(b-a\\) of all its points) and a line of slope \\(-2\\) removes **one**\nelement from \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Because we have exactly \\(k=2t+1\\) lines, after\nremoving \\(k\\) elements from each multiset the parity of the remaining\nmultisets must be even.  However, when \\(t\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge5\\)) the total\nnumber of distinct values in \\(\\mathcal D\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal S\\)) is\n\\(2t+1\\), which is odd; removing an odd number (\\(k\\)) of elements leaves an\nodd number of elements, contradicting the requirement that every point of\n\\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) be covered.  Hence **no configuration exists for odd\n\\(k\\ge5\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct,\n  none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line\n  \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical