            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   A vertical (or horizontal) line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points.  \n   Consequently, for any integer \\(m\\) the family of vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,m\\) covers the largest possible number of points among all families of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n3. **Reduction to a small triangle.**  \n   Suppose we want exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) except  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q_k:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal Q_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (Geometrically \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) is a translate of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).)  \n   Hence the problem is equivalent to covering the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **How many points can a sunny line contain inside \\(\\mathcal P_k\\)?**  \n   Write a sunny line in reduced integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   y-y_0=\\frac{p}{q}(x-x_0),\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;\n   \\gcd(|p|,|q|)=1,\\;p+q\\neq0 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All its lattice points are \\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\).  \n   When \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(|p+q|\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) the parameter \\(t\\) can therefore take at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   different values.  \n   Because a sunny line is not parallel to the forbidden directions, \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\).  \n   The right‑hand side of (5) is maximal when \\(|p+q|=1\\); in that case the bound equals \\(k+1\\), but the geometric shape of the triangle forces a stricter bound:\n\n   *If \\(|p+q|=1\\) then the direction vector is \\((q,p)=(\\pm1,\\mp2)\\) or \\((\\pm2,\\mp1)\\); in either case the line meets the triangle in at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points.*  \n\n   Hence **every sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) in at most**\n   \\[\n   M_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   points.  Equality in (6) occurs exactly when \\(|p+q|=2\\); the two possibilities are  \n\n   \\[\n   (p,q)=(1,1)\\;( \\text{slope }+1),\\qquad (p,q)=(-2,1)\\;(\\text{slope }-2),\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   i.e. the families \\(y=x+c\\) and \\(y=-2x+d\\).\n\n5. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines must together cover the \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).  Using (6),\n\n   \\[\n   k\\,M_k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is