t that can produce an odd‑sized class is a value of \\(d\\) with \\(|d|\\) odd.  There are exactly \\(t\\) such values of \\(d\\) (namely \\(\\pm1,\\pm3,\\dots ,\\pm(2t-1)\\)).  Hence at most \\(t\\) parts can be of slope +1.  The remaining \\(k-t= t+1\\) parts would have to be of slope ‑2, but the numbers of points with a fixed value of \\(s\\) are all **even** (they are of the form \\(\\frac{k+1-|s-(k+2)|}{2}\\) with \\(k+1\\) even).  Thus a slope ‑2 part can never have the required odd size – contradiction.\n\n   * If \\(k\\equiv 3\\pmod4\\) then \\(t\\) is odd and each part must have an even size \\(t+1\\).  Every class defined by a fixed \\(d\\) has odd cardinality (as above), so a slope +1 part can never have the required even size.  Hence no partition exists.\n\n   Therefore **no partition of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) into \\(k\\) optimal sunny lines exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\)**, and consequently such a configuration of lines does not exist.\n\n7. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  Add any optimal sunny line through that point, e.g. \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   – For \\(n=3\\) we can take the three lines  \n     \\[\n     L_1:x=1\\;( \\text{non‑sunny}),\\qquad\n     L_2:\\;y=2x-3\\;( \\text{sunny}),\\qquad\n     L_3:\\;y=3x-6\\;( \\text{sunny}),\n     \\]\n     which cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   – For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the three points left after the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines are \\((n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\).  No pair of these points lies on a sunny line (the only possible slopes are \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)), so at least three sunny lines are needed.  Hence **\\(k=2\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\).**\n\n   *\\(k=3\