\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   d=b-a,\\qquad s=2a+b .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Lines of slope \\(+1\\)* (the family \\(y=x+c\\)) have constant \\(d\\);  \n   *lines of slope \\(-2\\)* (the family \\(y=-2x+d\\)) have constant \\(s\\).\n\n   In \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) the multiplicities of the values of \\(d\\) and of \\(s\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\{(a,b):d\\}=k+1-|d|,\\qquad\n   \\#\\{(a,b):s\\}= \\begin{cases}\n   \\displaystyle\\frac{k+1-|s-(k+2)|}{2}, & 3\\le s\\le 2k+1,\\\\[2mm]\n   0, & \\text{otherwise}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(k=2t+1\\) the numbers \\(k+1-|d|\\) are **odd exactly when \\(|d|\\) is odd**; similarly the numbers in the second line of (10) are odd exactly when \\(s\\) is odd.  Because each line of the partition must consist entirely of points with the same invariant (\\(d\\) for slope +1, \\(s\\) for slope ‑2), each part of the partition must contain an odd number of points.\n\n   However the required size of each part is \\(t+1\\), which is **even** when \\(t\\) is odd (i.e. when \\(k\\equiv 3\\pmod 4\\)) and **odd** when \\(t\\) is even (i.e. when \\(k\\equiv 1\\pmod 4\\)).  In either case a contradiction arises:\n\n   * If \\(k\\equiv 1\\pmod4\\) then \\(t\\) is even and each part must have an odd size \\(t+1\\).  The only invariant that can produce an odd‑sized class is a value of \\(d\\) with \\(|d|\\) odd.  There are exactly \\(t\\) such values of \\(d\\) (namely \\(\\pm1,\\pm3,\\dots ,\\pm(2t-1)\\)).  Hence at most \\(t\\) parts can be of slope +1.  The remaining \\(k-t= t+1\\) parts would have to be of slope ‑2, but the numbers of points with a fixed value of \\(s\\) are all **even** (they are of the form \\(\\frac{k+1-|s-(k+2)|}{2}\\) with \\(k+1\\) even).  Thus a slope ‑2 part can never have the required odd size – contradiction.\n\n   * If \\(k\\equiv 3\\pmod4\\) then \\(t\\) is odd and each part must have an even size \\(t+1\\).  Every class defined by a fixed \\(d\\) has odd cardinality (as above), so a slope +1 part can never have the required even size.  Hence no partition exists.\n\n   Therefore **no partition of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) into \\(k\\) optimal sunny lines exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\)**, and consequently such a configuration of lines does not exist.\n\n7. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  Add any optimal sunny line through that point, e.g. \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   – For \\(n=3\\) we can take the three lines  \n     \\[\n     L_1:x=1\\;( \\text{non‑sunny}),\\qquad\n     L_2:\\;y=2x-3\\;( \\text{sunny}),\\qquad\n     L_3:\\;y=3x-6\\;( \\text{sunny}),\n     \\]\n     which cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   – For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the three points left after the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines are \\((n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\).  No pair of these points lies on a sunny line (the only possible slopes are \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)), so at least three sunny lines are needed.  Hence **\\(k=2\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\).**\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The remaining six points form the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_3\\):\n     \\[\n     (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n     \\]\n     They are covered by the three optimal sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y = x-(n-3) \\quad\\text{(slope }+1),\\\\\n     L_2 &: y = -2x + (2n+1) \\quad\\text{(slope }-2),\\\\\n     L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2} \\quad\\text{(slope }-\\tfrac12),\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     each of which contains exactly two of the six points.  Thus a configuration with exactly three sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *\\(k\\ge4\\).*  By the discussion in Sections 4–6, no configuration exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\) when \\(k\\ge4\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The analysis yields the complete list of admissible values of \\(k\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,1,3\\},   & n\\ge4 .\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\]\n\n   The three families of constructions above show that each listed value is indeed attainable, and the impossibility arguments rule out every other integer."
