"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   The points that are not yet covered form a translate of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to the following one:\n\n   > *For a given \\(k\\ge 0\\) decide whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct **sunny** lines.*\n\n2. **A bound for a sunny line (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   Let a sunny line be written in reduced integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   y-y_0=\\frac{p}{q}(x-x_0),\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;\n   \\gcd(|p|,|q|)=1,\\;p+q\\neq0 .\n   \\]\n\n   All lattice points on the line are \\((x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp)\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\).  \n   When \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(p+q\\).  \n   Because points of \\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\), the number of\n   admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must stay positive; this forces an additional\n   restriction that halves the bound when \\(|p+q|=1\\).  A short case analysis\n   (the direction vector moves at least one unit in each coordinate) shows\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   for every sunny line \\(L\\).  The bound is attained by the line\n   \\(y=-2x+(k+2)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)), which contains exactly\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n3. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   The set \\(T_k\\) has \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n   Using (2),\n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (3) is possible only when the floor equals \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\),\n   i.e. when \\(k\\) is **odd**.  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\text{ must be odd}} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Only the three odd values \\(k=1,3\\) are feasible (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n\n   *Maximum\u2011size sunny lines.*  \n   Equality in (3) forces every sunny line to contain the maximal number\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of points; otherwise the total\n   would be too small.  By Lemma\u202f1 this can happen only for lines with\n   \\(|p+q|=1\\).  The only such lines that actually attain the bound are the\n   lines of slope \\(-2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=-2x+d ,\\qquad d\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For the set \\(T_k\\) the line \\(L_{k+2}\\) is the **unique** line of this\n   family that meets the bound; all other lines of slope \\(-2\\) meet at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n   *Consequences.*  \n   If \\(k\\ge5\\) (still odd) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge3\\).\n   Because only the single line \\(L_{k+2}\\) can contain that many points,\n   at most one of the required \\(k\\) lines can be \u201clarge\u2019\u2019; the remaining\n   \\(k-1\\) lines would each contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n   points, and the total number of covered points would be\n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n   +(k-1)\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence no covering exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n   *The remaining odd values.*  \n   - \\(k=1\\): the single point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_1\\) is covered by any sunny\n     line through it (e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\)).  \n   - \\(k=3\\): the set \\(T_3\\) consists of six points.  T