\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\), the number of\n> admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> Moreover \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must stay positive; this forces an additional\n> restriction that halves the bound when \\(|p+q|=1\\).  A short case analysis\n> (the direction vector moves at least one unit in each coordinate) shows\n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\;|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;}\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> for every sunny line \\(L\\).  The bound is attained by the line\n> \\(y=-2x+(k+2)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)), which contains exactly\n> \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe argument that the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(p+q\\) per step is correct. However, the transition from (1) to the final bound (2) is not justified:\n\n* The inequality (1) is itself not tight: for example, with \\(k=5\\) and \\(|p+q|=4\\) the right‑hand side equals \\(\\lfloor6/4\\rfloor=1\\) while a line can actually contain two points of \\(T_5\\).  \n* The claim that “the additional restriction halves the bound when \\(|p+q|=1\\)” is asserted without proof.  \n* The “short case analysis” that supposedly yields \\(|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) is omitted; a rigorous proof would need to treat all possibilities for \\((p,q)\\) and the positivity constraints.  \n\nThus the bound (2) is plausible but not established, and the statement that it is attained only by the line \\(y=-2x+(k+2)\\) is also left without justification (other lines with \\(|p+q|=1\\) could a priori attain the same number of points).  \n**Classification:** Justification Gap.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Lemma 2 – necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “The set \\(T_k\\) has \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n> Using (2),  \n> \\[\n> k\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n> \\;\\ge\\;\n> \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> Equality in (3) is possible only when the floor equals \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\),\n> i.e. when \\(k\\) is **odd**.  Consequently  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{k\\text{ must be odd}} .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nGiven the bound (2), the inequality (3) follows directly, and the deduction that \\(k\\) must be odd is correct. The logical step is sound **provided** that bound (2) holds, which, as noted above, has not been proved. Hence this lemma inherits the justification gap from Lemma 1 but contains no additional error.  \n**Classification:** No new error (depends on previous gap).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma 3 – only \\(k=1,3\\) are feasible  \n\n**Quoted text (first part):**  \n> “Equality in (3) forces every sunny line to contain the maximal number\n> \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) of points; otherwise the total\n> would be too small.  By Lemma 1 this can happen only for lines with\n> \\(|p+q|=1\\).  The only such lines that actually attain the bound are the\n> lines of slope \\(-2\\),  \n> \\[\n> L_d:\\;y=-2x+d ,\\qquad d\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe deduction that equality forces each line to be “maximal” is correct. However:\n\n* The statement “by Lemma 1 this can happen only for lines with \\(|p+q|=1\\)” follows from the (unproved) bound (2); it is not shown that a line with \\(|p+q|>1\\) cannot also achieve the maximal number of points (though this is plausible, a proof is missing).  \n* The claim that *only* the slope \\(-2\\) lines attain the bound is not justified. There are other integer pairs \\((p,q)\\) with \\(|p+q|=1\\) (e.g. \\((p,q)=(3,-2)\\) giving slope \\(-3/2\\)). It must be shown that none of these can contain \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\); this argument is omitted.  \n\nThus this part contains a justification gap.\n\n**Quoted text (second part):**  \n> “If \\(k\\ge5\\) (still odd) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge3\\).\n> Because only the single line \\(L_{k+2}\\) can contain that many points,\n> at most one of the required \\(k\\) lines can be “large’’; the remaining\n> \\(k-1\\) lines would each contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, and the total number of covered points would be  \n> \\[\n> \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n> +(k-1)\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n> <\\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n> \\]  \n> a contradiction.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe inequality is correct **if** the premise “only the single line \\(L_{k+2}\\) can contain \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points” holds. As noted, the uniqueness of this “large” line has not been proved; other lines of slope \\(-2\\) with different intercepts could also contain the same number of points for certain \\(k\\). Without a rigorous argument establishing the uniqueness, the conclusion is not justified.  \n**Classification:** Justification Gap.\n\n**Quoted text (remaining odd values):**  \n> “- \\(k=1\\): …  \n> - \\(k=3\\): … three sunny lines … each contains exactly two points of \\(T_3\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe explicit covering for \\(k=1\\) is correct. The covering for \\(k=3\\) of the set \\(T_3\\) (the three lines \\(y=x\\), \\(y=-2x+5\\), \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\)) is correct and can be checked directly. No error here.  \n\n---\n\n### 5. Putting everything together – existence constructions  \n\n**Quoted text (k=0):**  \n> “\\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non‑sunny.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nCorrect; these lines cover every point because the first coordinate of any point satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n\n**Quoted text (k=1):**  \n> “\\(k=1\\): use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add any sunny line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\)).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\le n-1\\). The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); a sunny line through it indeed exists. The example line \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) because \\(-2n+2n+1=1\\). The line is not parallel to the forbidden directions, so it is sunny. Construction is valid.  \n\n**Quoted text (k=3 – the problematic part):**  \n> “\\(k=3\\): use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and the three sunny lines displayed in Lemma 3 (translated by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\)).  The three translated lines are  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> y &= x-(n-3),\\\\\n> y &= -2x+(2n+1),\\\\\n> y &= -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe intended translation of the three lines that cover \\(T_3\\) is obtained by replacing \\(x\\) with \\(x-(n-3)\\) in each original equation.  \n\n* For \\(y=x\\) the translation yields \\(y = x-(n-3)\\) – correct.  \n* For \\(y=-2x+5\\) the translation yields  \n  \\[\n  y = -2\\bigl(x-(n-3)\\bigr)+5 = -2x + 2n -1,\n  \\]  \n  i.e. the constant term should be \\(2n-1\\). The solution writes \\(2n+1\\), which is **off by 2**.  \n* For \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) the translation gives  \n  \\[\n  y = -\\tfrac12\\bigl(x-(n-3)\\bigr)+\\tfrac52 = -\\tfrac12x + \\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n  \\]  \n  which is correctly stated.\n\nBecause the second line is incorrect, the three lines no longer cover the required six points of the translated triangle. A concrete counter‑example (e.g. \\(n=5\\)) shows that points \\((3,3)\\) and \\((4,1)\\) remain uncovered. Hence the existence proof for \\(k=3\\) fails as written.  \n\n**Classification:** Critical Error – the construction does not work, invalidating the claimed existence for \\(k=3\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Final conclusion  \n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Hence the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0,1,3\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe conclusion relies on the (unproved) equivalence to covering \\(T_k\\), on the unproved bound for sunny lines, on the unproved uniqueness of the “large” line, and on the faulty construction for \\(k=3\\). Since several of these foundational steps are either unjustified or outright false, the conclusion is not established.  \n\n**Classification:** The conclusion is unsupported due to the accumulated gaps and the critical error.  \n\n--- \n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect translated line for the \\(k=3\\) construction) and multiple **Justification Gaps** (equivalence reduction, bound derivation, uniqueness arguments). Consequently the solution does not constitute a valid proof of the problem’s answer."
