 containing integer points.\nWrite it in the integer form  \n\n\\[\np x-q y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq q,\\;\n\\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lattice points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_i,y_i)=(x_0+i q,\\;y_0+i p),\\qquad i\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums satisfy  \n\n\\[\ns_i:=x_i+y_i=(x_0+y_0)+i(p+q).                     \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause points of \\(T_k\\) obey \\(2\\le s_i\\le k+1\\), the number of admissible\nindices \\(i\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).*  \nFrom (7) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Case \\(|p+q|=1\\).*  \nThen \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  Since \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and both \\(p,q\\) are non‑zero,\nnecessarily \\(|q|=1\\) and \\(|p|=2\\); the direction vector is\n\\((q,p)=(-1,2)\\) or \\((1,-2)\\).  Using (5) together with the constraints\n\\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le k+1\\) one obtains\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8) and (9) yields the uniform estimate\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }L.                     \\tag{Lemma 1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Consequences for a covering\n\nAssume that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).  Let \\(m_i\\) be the number of\npoints of \\(T_k\\) on the \\(i\\)-th line.  Then\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = |T_k| = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .          \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1, \\(m_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2t\\); then\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and (11) holds with\nstrict inequality, giving no restriction.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2t+1\\); then\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and (11) becomes an\nequality.  Consequently **every** sunny line must attain the maximal\npossible number of points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_k|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Structure of a maximal sunny line  \n\nEquality in Lemma 1 forces \\(|p+q|=1\\) and \\(|q|=1\\); thus the direction\nvector is either \\((-1,2)\\) or \\((1,-2)\\).  The corresponding slopes are\n\\(-2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) (direction \\((1,1)\\)) also\nsatisfies (12) when \\(k\\) is odd, because then\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\nHence any sunny line that contains the maximal number of points of\n\\(T_k\\) is of one of the three types\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+c,\\qquad y=\\tfrac12 x +c } .\n\\tag{Lemma 3}\n\\]\n\nFor each of these families the quantity \\(a-b\\) (with \\((a,b)\\) a point on\nthe line) is constant modulo \\(3\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny=x      &\\Longrightarrow a-b\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\\\\ny=-2x+c  &\\Longrightarrow a-b\\equiv r\\pmod3\\ (\\text{constant }r),\\\\\ny=\\tfrac12x+c &\\Longrightarrow a-b\\equiv r\\pmod3 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus **every maximal sunny line lies entirely inside a single residue class\nof \\(a-b\\pmod3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility for odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) is partitioned by the three residue classes of \\(a-b\\pmod3\\).\nLet \\(N_r\\) be the number of points of \\(T_k\\) belonging to class \\(r\\)\n(\\(r=0,1,2\\)).  Any covering by sunny lines uses at most three distinct\nclasses, because each line stays inside one class (13).\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\ge5\\), Lemma 2 tells us that all \\(k\\) lines must be\nmaximal, hence each line is of one of the three types listed in Lemma 3\nand therefore confined to a single class.  With \\(k\\ge5\\) the pigeonhole\nprinciple forces at least two lines to belong to the same class, which\nimplies that those two lines share at least one point of \\(T_k\\).  Hence the\ntotal number of *distinct* points covered is **strictly less** than\n\\(\\sum_i m_i = |T_k|\\), contradicting (10).  Therefore no covering exists\nfor odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. The remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n  non‑sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only\n  uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); any sunny line through this point works,\n  e.g. \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\).  After removing two vertical lines the uncovered set is\n  \\(T_2=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\).  Any two of these points are\n  collinear with a non‑sunny direction (horizontal, vertical or slope\n  \\(-1\\)), so a sunny line can contain at most one of them.  Hence two\n  sunny lines cannot cover all three points; \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n* \\(k=3\\).  Remove the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); the remaining\n  points form \\(T_3\\), which consists of the six points  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n  \\]\n\n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1&: y = x-(n-3),\\\\\n  L_2&: y = -2x+(2n-1),\\\\\n  L_3&: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\tag{14}\n  \\]\n\n  are obtained by translating the three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n  \\]\n\n  (which cover \\(T_3\\)) by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\).  Each of the lines in\n  (14) contains exactly two of the points of \\(T_3\\); together they cover\n  all six points.  Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration\n  of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones that satisfies the\n  required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Conclusion\n\nThe analysis shows that the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions\nexists are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3}.\n\\]\n\nThese values are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\), and all other values are\nimpossible."
