                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular lattice set.**  \n   Replace the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   After a horizontal translation the uncovered points become  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to  \n\n   > **Problem \\((P_k)\\).**  Can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines?\n\n2. **A universal bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Write a sunny line in the integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   p x-q y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q,\\;\n   \\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All lattice points on the line are  \n\n   \\[\n   (x_t,y_t)=(x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Their sums satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   s_t:=x_t+y_t=(x_0+y_0)+t(p+q).                     \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because points of \\(T_k\\) obey \\(2\\le s_t\\le k+1\\), the number of admissible\n   integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .      \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\)*, (5) gives \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \n\n   *If \\(|p+q|=1\\)*, then \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  In this case \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have opposite\n   signs, so when the sum increases by \\(1\\) one coordinate decreases by at\n   least \\(1\\).  Starting from the smallest admissible sum \\(2\\), the\n   decreasing coordinate can stay positive for at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps; consequently the line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\).  \n\n   Combining the two cases we obtain the uniform estimate  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n   \\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }L .\n   \\tag{Lemma 1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Consequences for a covering of \\(T_k\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).  Let \\(m_i\\) be the number of points of\n   \\(T_k\\) on the \\(i\\)-th line.  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = |T_k| = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .          \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1, \\(m_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2t\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and (8) becomes\n   \\(2t(t+1)\\ge t(2t+1)\\), which is always true; no restriction follows.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2t+1\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and (8) is an equality:\n   \\[\n   (2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence **every** line must attain the maximal possible number of points,\n   i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   |L_i\\cap T_k|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\qquad\\text{for all }i .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Structure of a maximal sunny line.**  \n   Equality in Lemma 1 forces \\(|p+q|=1\\).  With \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p,q\\neq0\\) this yields the three possibilities  \n\n   \\[\n   (p,q)=(1,0)\\;( \\text{vertical, not sunny}),\\;\n   (p,q)=(1,1)\\;(y=x),\\;\n   (p,q)=(2,-1)\\;(y=-2x+\\text{const}),\\;\n   (p,q)=(-1,2)\\;(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\text{const}).\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical case is excluded by the definition of a sunny line, so the\n   only sunny families that can achieve the bound are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+c,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+c } .\n   \\tag{Lemma 2}\n   \\]\n\n   For each of these families the quantity \\(a-b\\) (with \\((a,b)\\) a point on the line) is constant modulo \\(3\\):\n   \\[\n   y=x\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a-b\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\qquad\n   y=-2x+c\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a-b\\equiv c\\pmod3,\\qquad\n   y=-\\tfrac12x+c\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a-b\\equiv c\\pmod3 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently **every maximal sunny line lies entirely inside a single residue\n   class of \\(a-b\\pmod3\\).**\n\n5. **Impossibility for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_k\\) is partitioned by the three residue classes of \\(a-b\\pmod3\\).\n   Because each maximal line stays inside one class, at most three distinct\n   maximal lines can be pairwise disjoint inside \\(T_k\\).  If \\(k\\) is odd and\n   \\(k\\ge5\\), (9) forces all \\(k\\) lines to be maximal, which would require at\n   least \\(k\\) distinct residue classes – impossible.  Hence no covering exists\n   for odd \\(k\\ge5\\).\n\n6. **The remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny\n   and they clearly cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only\n   uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); any sunny line through this point works,\n   e.g. \\(y=-2x+2n+1\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  After removing two vertical lines the uncovered set is\n   \\(T_2=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}\\).  Any two of these points are collinear\n   with a non‑sunny direction (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)), so a\n   sunny line can contain at most one of them.  Therefore two sunny lines cannot\n   cover all three points; \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Remove the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); the remaining\n   points form \\(T_3\\), which consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&:\\; y = x-(n-3),\\\\\n   L_2&:\\; y = -2x+(2n-1),\\\\\n   L_3&:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   are obtained by translating the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n   \\]\n\n   (which cover \\(T_3\\)) by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\).  Each of the lines in (11)\n   contains exactly two points of \\(T_3\\); together they cover all six points.\n   Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines with exactly three sunny ones that satisfies the required covering\n   condition.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The analysis shows that the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a\n   configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions\n   exists are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3}.\n   \\]\n\n   These values are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\), and all other values are\n   impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines\n\\(x= \\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\) and \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the set \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume that among the required \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\nReplace the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed\n\\(n-k\\).  Translating the whole picture left by \\(n-k\\) units (i.e. sending\n\\((x,y)\\mapsto (x-(n-k),y)\\)) does not affect the property “a line is sunny”.\nAfter the translation the uncovered points are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following.\n\n> **Problem \\((P_k)\\).**  Can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct\n> sunny lines?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A universal bound for a sunny line (Lemma 1)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains lattice points.  It can be written as  \n\n\\[\np x-q y=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;p,q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q,\\;\n\\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n(The conditions \\(p,q\\neq0\\) exclude horizontals and verticals; the condition\n\\(p\\neq -q\\) excludes lines parallel to \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).)\n\nAll integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_t,y_t)=(x_0+tq,\\;y_0+tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z ,\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\nwhere \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is any fixed lattice point on \\(L\\).\n\nTheir sums satisfy  \n\n\\[\ns_t:=x_t+y_t=(x_0+y_0)+t(p+q).                     \\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nHence the set \\(\\{s_t\\}\\) is an arithmetic progression with common difference\n\\(d:=|p+q|\\).\n\nA point of \\(T_k\\) must obey \\(2\\le s_t\\le k+1\\); consequently the number of\nadmissible integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{d}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .          \\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(d\\ge2\\)*, (18) gives \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n*If \\(d=1\\)*, then \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  In this case \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have opposite\nsigns (otherwise \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\)).  Assume \\(p>0,\\;q<0\\) (the other sign\nchoice is symmetric).  From (16) we see that when the sum increases by \\(1\\)\nthe \\(x\\)-coordinate decreases by \\(|q|\\ge1\\).  Starting from the smallest\nadmissible sum \\(2\\), the \\(x\\)-coordinate can stay positive for at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps; the same bound holds when\n\\(p<0,\\;q>0\\).  Therefore a line with \\(d=1\\) contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\nCombining the two cases we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|L\\cap T_k|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}\n\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }L .\n\\tag{Lemma 1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Consequences for a covering (Lemma 2)\n\nSuppose \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).  Let \\(m_i\\) be the number of points\nof \\(T_k\\) on the \\(i\\)-th line.  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = |T_k| = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .          \\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1, \\(m_i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2t\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\)\nand (21) becomes \\(2t(t+1)\\ge t(2t+1)\\), which is always true; no further\nrestriction follows.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2t+1\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\)\nand (21) is an equality.  Consequently **every** line must attain the maximal\npossible number of points, i.e.\n\n\\[\n|L_i\\cap T_k|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\qquad\\text{for all }i .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Structure of a maximal sunny line (Lemma 3)\n\nEquality in Lemma 1 forces \\(d=|p+q|=1\\).  With \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and\n\\(p,q\\neq0\\) the only possibilities for \\((p,q)\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(p,q)=(1,1),\\;(2,-1),\\;(-1,2) .\n\\]\n\nThese give the three families of sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+c,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+c } .\n\\tag{Lemma 3}\n\\]\n\nFor each family the quantity \\(a-b\\) is constant modulo \\(3\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny=x      &\\Longrightarrow a-b\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\\\\ny=-2x+c  &\\Longrightarrow a-b\\equiv c\\pmod3,\\\\\ny=-\\tfrac12x+c &\\Longrightarrow a-b\\equiv c\\pmod3 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThus a maximal sunny line lies completely inside a single residue class of\n\\(a-b\\pmod3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. No covering for odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) is partitioned by the three residue classes of\n\\(a-b\\pmod3\\).  Because each maximal line stays inside one class, at most three\npairwise disjoint maximal lines can exist inside \\(T_k\\).  If \\(k\\) is odd\nand \\(k\\ge5\\), (22) forces **all** \\(k\\) lines to be maximal, which would\nrequire at least \\(k\\) distinct residue classes – impossible.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No covering exists for odd }k\\ge5.}\n\\tag{Lemma 4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. The remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  They are non‑sunny and\nobviously cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only point not yet\ncovered is \\((n,1)\\).  The line  \n\n\\[\ny=-2x+2n+1\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((n,1)\\) and is sunny (its slope \\(-2\\) is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Thus we have a configuration with exactly one sunny\nline.\n\n*\\(k=2\\).*  \nAfter removing two vertical lines the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nT_2=\\{(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny two of these points are collinear with a non‑sunny direction:\n\\((n-1,1)-(n-1,2)\\) is vertical, \\((n-1,1)-(n,1)\\) is horizontal,\n\\((n-1,2)-(n,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most\none of the three points, and two sunny lines cannot cover all of them.\nTherefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nRemove the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\); the remaining points are\nexactly the set \\(T_3\\), i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\; y = x-(n-3),\\\\\nL_2&:\\; y = -2x+(2n-1),\\\\\nL_3&:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nare obtained by translating the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\]\n\n(which cover \\(T_3\\)) by the vector \\((n-3,0)\\).  Each line in (25) contains\nexactly two points of \\(T_3\\); together they cover all six points.  Adding the\n\\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly\nthree sunny ones that satisfies the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Final answer\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3}.\n\\]\n\nThese values are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\), and all other values\nare impossible. ∎"
